TI  - Pre-departure ship fuel consumption prediction under out-of-distribution condition
T2  - EXPERT SYSTEMS WITH APPLICATIONS
LA  - English
KW  - Green shipping
KW  - Ship fuel consumption prediction
KW  - Domain generalization
KW  - Voyage covariate shift
KW  - Recurrent neural network
KW  - OPTIMIZATION
AB  - Shipping plays a critical role in global trade but is also a major source of greenhouse gas emissions. To address this issue, the International Maritime Organization has introduced stringent emission reduction targets, increasing the importance of fuel-efficient operational strategies. While fuel consumption prediction models have been widely studied, most assume that training and test data are independently and identically distributed (i.i.d.) and often rely on engine-related variables that are unavailable prior to departure. These assumptions violate real-world operational conditions, leading to out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization failure in unseen voyages and limiting the practical utility of existing models. This paper introduces the concept of voyage covariate shift (VCS), defined as an inter-voyage distributional shift that degrades pre-departure prediction performance. To address VCS, we propose the Voyage-aware Recurrent Neural Network (VaRNN), which disentangles voyage-invariant and task-specific representations and applies a commonality loss to align only the invariant component across voyages. VaRNN is evaluated using voyage-based cross-validation, a protocol designed to prevent data leakage and to fairly assess generalization under VCS. Experimental results show that VaRNN achieved the lowest mean root mean squared error and mean absolute error across five train-validation splits, demonstrating superior average performance and stable generalization under VCS. In addition to improving prediction accuracy, we quantitatively verified the effectiveness of the commonality loss through ablation studies.
