TI  - A two-stage semi-supervised domain generalization network for fault diagnosis under unknown working conditions
T2  - RELIABILITY ENGINEERING & SYSTEM SAFETY
LA  - English
KW  - Fault diagnosis
KW  - Semi-supervised domain generalization
KW  - Rotating machinery
KW  - Deep learning
AB  - In recent years, domain generalization methods have gradually emerged, aiming to mitigate the impact of domain shifts and enhance the model's generalization ability under unseen working conditions. However, domain generalization-based fault diagnosis methods face challenges due to the difficulty of obtaining sufficient high-quality labeled data, and improving the generalization capability of the models remains an urgent issue. To address these challenges, a two-stage semi-supervised domain generalization network is proposed, which integrates pseudo-label generation with domain generalization learning, addressing the problem of label scarcity while enhancing the generalization capability of models in industrial fault diagnosis. In the pseudo-labeling stage, domain adaptation strategies are employed to assign pseudo-labels to unlabeled source domain samples, addressing the issue of limited labeled data. In the generalization stage, discriminative-enhanced domaininvariant feature mixup and large margin loss are used to improve the diversity of features and reduce synthetic noise. Sharpness-aware minimization gradient matching is applied to achieve more robust optimization and reduce the risk of overfitting. Additionally, a domain label smoothing strategy is introduced to reduce overclassification by the domain discriminator. Experimental results show that the proposed model achieves higher diagnostic accuracy and greater stability compared to existing state-of-the-art semi-supervised domain generalization methods on two bearing datasets.
